This case does not pin the guest's `lane1 ...` trailer.

Why (measured 2026-07-29, 10 boots per case on one machine):
  boot-cross-core-ring-full   2/10 reproduce the pinned trailer
  every other boot-cross-core-* case, boot-cores-2, boot-cores-3   10/10

The test's own assertions (`admitted == 1`, `rejected == 1`) are
deterministic and still pinned -- they are what this golden exists to
prove, and they pass every run. Only the Lane 1 counters are dropped.

Root cause, recorded in stdlib/core/runtime.wr above `__wrela_rt_drain`:
this case asserts a *rejection*, so it deliberately halts with one
admitted-but-unprocessed message outstanding. The Lane 1 counters are
written by whichever core drains (`__wrela_rt_enqueue` / `rt_select` /
`rt_run_one`) and read by core 0 at `__wrela_rt_summary_and_halt`, with no
happens-before edge between them -- so whether core 1 was scheduled to
drain first is pure host timing. plans/M15.md item I deleted the Condvar
baton that used to serialize this; item J, the interleaving enumerator
that would have made it reproducible, was cut.

The pin was already re-pinned once to a different draw of the same race
(a2579154 pinned `run_one=0 hits=`; 82314ed6 changed it to `run_one=1
hits=0:1`), which is what a pin chasing nondeterminism looks like.

Delete this marker when either remedy runtime.wr names lands:
  * a bounded quiesce-before-halt step in the entry driver, or
  * per-core Lane 1 striping summed at dump (the counters are also a
    non-atomic read-modify-write on shared @placed state today, so two
    cores draining concurrently can lose an increment even with a join).
